.PP
zshrs-only builtins are listed under \fBzshrs\-only builtins\fR below; they appear in
.B builtin
and
.B "whence -m"
output.
.SS "Status banner: zbanner"
.B zbanner
prints the ZSHRS logo, a box with the version and the builtin and
extension totals, and one live line: the daemon socket and whether it
answers (\fB// up\fR, \fB// no daemon running\fR or
\fB// daemon disabled\fR), then this shell's function, alias, parameter
and job counts \(em the counts zsh's own
.BR $functions ,
.BR $aliases " + " $galiases ,
.B $parameters
and
.B $jobstates
report.  It takes no arguments (exit status 2 if given one) and never
prints at startup.
.B "zshrs \-\-banner"
prints the same from outside a shell, without the shell counts.
.SS "Language model: ai"
.B ai
[\fB\-m\fR \fImodel\fR] [\fB\-s\fR \fIsystem\fR] [\fB\-P\fR \fIprovider\fR]
[\fB\-t\fR \fImax-tokens\fR] [\fB\-T\fR \fItemp\fR] [\fB\-o\fR \fItimeout\fR]
[\fB\-nkb\fR] [\fB\-v\fR \fIvar\fR | \fB\-a\fR \fIarray\fR] [\fB\-\-\fR] \fIprompt\fR...
.br
.B ai
\fB\-c\fR | \fB\-K\fR | \fB\-H\fR | \fB\-G\fR | \fB\-S\fR \fIkey\fR=\fIvalue\fR |
\fB\-M\fR \fIpattern\fR=\fIresponse\fR
.PP
Calls a language model from inside the shell process (a port of strykelang's
.BR ai ).
The response streams to stdout as it arrives;
.B \-b
buffers it,
.B \-v
assigns it to a scalar and
.B \-a
to an array, one line per element.  With no prompt words, or a lone
.BR \- ,
the prompt is read from stdin.
.B \-n
bypasses the in-process response cache and
.B \-k
marks the system prompt for Anthropic prompt caching.
.B \-c
prints the cost and token totals,
.B \-H
the recent calls (tab-separated),
.B \-G
the current configuration;
.B \-K
clears the cache,
.B \-S
changes a session default, and
.B \-M
installs a mock: a prompt matching the regex answers without a request.
.PP
Defaults come from
.B [ai]
in
.IR ~/.zshrs/zshrs.toml :
.B provider
(anthropic, openai, local, ollama, gemini; default anthropic),
.B model
(\%claude-opus-5),
.B api_key_env
(ANTHROPIC_API_KEY \(em the variable's name, not the key),
.BR base_url ,
.B cache
(true),
.B max_cost_run
(5.0 USD for the shell process; 0 disables),
.B max_tokens
(4096) and
.B timeout
(120 seconds).  Exit status 0 on success, 1 when the call failed (no key,
HTTP or transport error, cost ceiling), 2 for bad flags; the reason is written
to stderr as
.BR "zshrs: ai: " \fIreason\fR.
.SS "zshrs-only builtins"
Builtins that exist in zshrs but not in zsh, dispatched
in-process, with no fork.
.TP
.B arch
Print the machine architecture (uname \-m equivalent): \fBx86_64\fR, \fBarm64\fR, \fBaarch64\fR, etc.
.TP
.B async
Run a shell command string on the worker pool and print a job ID at once. \fBid=$(async 'cmd args')\fR; collect the output and status later with \fBawait $id\fR.
.TP
.B await
Block until the async job with the given ID finishes, print its captured stdout and return its exit status. \fBawait $id\fR; the ID is required.
.TP
.B barrier
Run commands in parallel and wait for all of them. \fBbarrier 'cmd1' ::: 'cmd2' ::: 'cmd3'\fR; returns the highest exit status.
.TP
.B base64
Encode / decode Base64. \fB\-d\fR decodes; \fB\-w0\fR no line wrap. coreutils drop\-in.
.TP
.B caller
Bash\-compatible \fBcaller\fR builtin. With no arg or 0: prints \fBLINE FUNC\fR for the current frame; with N>0: \fBLINE FUNC FILE\fR for the Nth call\-stack frame.
.TP
.B cdreplay
Replay the \fBcompdef\fR calls deferred while completion was not yet initialised (zinit turbo mode). \fBcdreplay [\-q]\fR; \fB\-q\fR replays quietly.
.TP
.B cksum
Print CRC32 checksum + byte count of each file. coreutils drop\-in.
.TP
.B comm
Compare two sorted files line\-by\-line. \fB\-1\fR / \fB\-2\fR / \fB\-3\fR suppress columns. coreutils drop\-in.
.TP
.B compgen
Bash\-compatible word generator. \fBcompgen \-W 'foo bar baz' fo\fR \(-> \fBfoo\fR. Used by bash\-completion scripts ported to zshrs.
.TP
.B cut
Extract fields or character ranges. \fB\-d':' \-f1,3\fR / \fB\-c5\-10\fR. coreutils drop\-in.
.TP
.B date
Print or set the system date. \fB+%FORMAT\fR strftime; \fB\-d 'rel'\fR parse relative; \fB\-u\fR UTC. coreutils drop\-in.
.TP
.B dbview
Dump the local zshrs SQLite mirror tables (autoload bodies, completion mirror, history FTS). Mirrors only &mdash; the authoritative cache is the rkyv\-mmap'd shard set; SQLite is hydrated read\-only for SQL/\fBdbview\fR inspection. \fBdbview \-\-table autoloads\fR filters by table.
.TP
.B dircolors
Emit \fBLS_COLORS\fR from a \fB.dircolors\fR file. coreutils drop\-in.
.TP
.B doctor
Diagnostic report of shell health \(em cache stats, autoload coverage, fpath sanity, daemon presence, memory footprint, recent error summary. zshrs\-only.
.TP
.B env
Run a command in a modified environment, or print the current environment. \fBenv \-i\fR empties; \fBenv VAR=val cmd\fR sets. coreutils drop\-in.
.TP
.B factor
Print prime factors. \fBfactor 60\fR \(-> \fB60: 2 2 3 5\fR. coreutils drop\-in.
.TP
.B find
Walk the filesystem and print / act on matches. Supports \fB\-name\fR/\fB\-type\fR/\fB\-mtime\fR/\fB\-exec\fR. coreutils drop\-in (subset).
.TP
.B fold
Wrap each input line to a width. \fB\-w N\fR width, \fB\-s\fR break at spaces. coreutils drop\-in.
.TP
.B hashinfo
Print hash\-table statistics for the shell's internal tables (the ZSH_HASH_DEBUG builtin of zsh).
.TP
.B head
Print the first N lines (\fB\-n N\fR) or bytes (\fB\-c N\fR) of each file. coreutils drop\-in.
.TP
.B help
Print help for a builtin. \fBhelp cd\fR shows the cd usage. zshrs\-only.
.TP
.B hostname
Print the system hostname. \fB\-s\fR short, \fB\-f\fR FQDN.
.TP
.B id
Print user / group IDs. \fB\-u\fR user only, \fB\-g\fR group only, \fB\-n\fR names. coreutils drop\-in.
.TP
.B intercept
Register an AOP intercept. \fBintercept before|after|around <cmd> { body }\fR runs \fBbody\fR around every invocation of \fB<cmd>\fR. The body is stored as source and compiled on each fire. \fB$INTERCEPT_NAME\fR, \fB$INTERCEPT_ARGS\fR, \fB$INTERCEPT_CMD\fR are bound; \fBafter\fR adds \fB$INTERCEPT_MS\fR, \fB$INTERCEPT_US\fR, \fB$INTERCEPT_STATUS\fR. zshrs\-only.
.TP
.B intercept_proceed
Inside an \fBaround\fR intercept body, invoke the underlying command. Required so the intercept doesn't shadow the call permanently.
.TP
.B logname
Print the user's login name. coreutils drop\-in.
.TP
.B mem
Print memory\-allocator statistics (the ZSH_MEM_DEBUG builtin of zsh).
.TP
.B mkfifo
Create named pipes (FIFOs). \fBmkfifo path ...\fR. coreutils drop\-in.
.TP
.B mktemp
Create a temp file or directory with a unique name. \fB\-d\fR directory, \fB\-p DIR\fR parent. coreutils drop\-in.
.TP
.B nameref
Declare a ksh93\-style named reference; same handler as \fBtypeset \-n\fR.
.TP
.B nice
Run a command with adjusted scheduling priority. \fBnice \-n 10 cmd\fR. coreutils drop\-in.
.TP
.B nl
Number lines. \fB\-b a\fR numbers all, \fB\-w N\fR field width. coreutils drop\-in.
.TP
.B nproc
Print the number of processing units available. \fB\-\-all\fR ignores affinity.
.TP
.B patdebug
Dump the compiled form of a pattern (the ZSH_PAT_DEBUG builtin of zsh). \fBpatdebug [\-p] PATTERN\fR.
.TP
.B peach
Parallel for\-each on the worker pool: run the template once per item with \fB{}\fR replaced by the item, printing output as each finishes. \fBpeach 'convert {} {}.png' *.svg\fR.
.TP
.B pgrep
Print PIDs of processes matching a pattern. \fB\-f\fR matches full command line.
.TP
.B pmap
Parallel map on the worker pool: run the template once per item with \fB{}\fR replaced by the item, printing output in input order. \fBpmap 'gzip {}' *.log\fR. Not the process memory\-map command of the same name.
.TP
.B printenv
Print the value of one or more environment variables, or all if none given. coreutils drop\-in.
.TP
.B profile
Profile command execution in\-process with nanosecond timing. \fBprofile CMD...\fR profiles a command line, \fBprofile \-s SCRIPT\fR a script string, \fBprofile \-f FUNC [ARGS]\fR a function call; \fB\-\-dump\fR shows and \fB\-\-clear\fR resets the accumulated data.
.TP
.B provenance
Value lineage over bytecode execution. \fBprovenance \-m NAME\fR arms tracking for a parameter; \fBprovenance NAME\fR then prints where its bytes came from (\fB$(...)\fR, \fB<(...)\fR, glob, heredoc, an earlier assignment) and every bytecode op that produced or consumed them \(em \fBassign\fR, \fBexpand\fR, \fBconcat\fR, \fBexec\fR, \fBcall\fR, \fBunset\fR \(em each with its \fB$LINENO\fR. \fB\-j\fR emits JSON, \fB\-u\fR untracks, \fB\-c\fR clears. Records nothing until armed; disable entirely with \fB[provenance] enabled = false\fR in \fB~/.zshrs/zshrs.toml\fR or \fBZSHRS_PROVENANCE=0\fR.
.TP
.B realpath
Resolve symlinks and \fB.\fR / \fB..\fR to a canonical absolute path. coreutils drop\-in.
.TP
.B rev
Reverse each input line character\-by\-character. coreutils drop\-in.
.TP
.B run_tests
Alias for \fBztest_run\fR \(em print the per\-block test summary and roll the per\-block counters into the run\-wide totals. Returns 0 on all\-pass, 1 if any assertion failed. Port of strykelang's \fBtest_run\fR / \fBrun_tests\fR builtin pair.
.TP
.B seq
Print a sequence of numbers. \fBseq 1 10\fR / \fBseq 1 2 10\fR / \fBseq \-w 1 10\fR. coreutils drop\-in.
.TP
.B sha256sum
Print or check SHA\-256 digests. \fB\-c FILE\fR checks. coreutils drop\-in.
.TP
.B shuf
Shuffle input lines. \fB\-n N\fR limit, \fB\-e ITEM...\fR shuffle args, \fB\-i LO\-HI\fR shuffle range. coreutils drop\-in.
.TP
.B sleep
Pause for the given duration. \fBsleep 1\fR, \fBsleep 0.5\fR, \fBsleep 1m\fR. coreutils drop\-in.
.TP
.B sum
BSD/sysv checksum + 1K\-block count. coreutils drop\-in.
.TP
.B tac
Concatenate files in reverse line order. coreutils drop\-in.
.TP
.B tail
Print the last N lines (\fB\-n N\fR) or follow appends (\fB\-f\fR). coreutils drop\-in.
.TP
.B tee
Copy stdin to stdout AND to each named file. \fB\-a\fR append. coreutils drop\-in.
.TP
.B touch
Create a file or update its mtime. \fB\-d STR\fR set time, \fB\-r REF\fR copy from REF. coreutils drop\-in.
.TP
.B tput
Terminal\-capability query. \fBtput cols\fR, \fBtput setaf 1\fR. Reads \fB$TERM\fR via terminfo.
.TP
.B tr
Translate / squeeze / delete characters. \fBtr a\-z A\-Z\fR uppercases. coreutils drop\-in.
.TP
.B tsort
Topological sort of partial\-order pairs read from stdin. coreutils drop\-in.
.TP
.B tty
Print the controlling terminal device path, or \fBnot a tty\fR if stdin isn't one.
.TP
.B uname
Print system info. \fB\-a\fR all, \fB\-s\fR kernel, \fB\-m\fR machine, \fB\-r\fR release. coreutils drop\-in.
.TP
.B unexpand
Convert leading spaces to tabs. \fB\-a\fR all spaces. coreutils drop\-in.
.TP
.B unlink
Remove a single file via the \fBunlink(2)\fR syscall (no \fB\-r\fR, no prompts). coreutils drop\-in.
.TP
.B users
Print the login names of users currently logged in.
.TP
.B wc
Count newlines, words, bytes. \fB\-l\fR lines, \fB\-w\fR words, \fB\-c\fR bytes. coreutils drop\-in.
.TP
.B whoami
Print the effective user name. coreutils drop\-in.
.TP
.B yes
Repeatedly output a line. \fByes\fR prints \fBy\fR forever; \fByes STR\fR prints STR. coreutils drop\-in.
.TP
.B zask
Ask another shell for input through the daemon and manage the requests. \fBzask \-\-target shell:N picker|input|dialog|menu [opts]\fR, \fBzask progress\fR, \fBzask pending\fR, \fBzask take\fR, \fBzask dismiss ID | \-\-all\fR, \fBzask inbox\-clear\fR, \fBzask response ID DATA\fR.
.TP
.B zassert_contains
\fBzassert_contains HAYSTACK NEEDLE [MSG]\fR \(em pass when NEEDLE is a substring of HAYSTACK. Bumps the per\-block \fBztest_pass_count\fR on success, \fBztest_fail_count\fR on failure; the OK/FAIL glyph + label print on stderr. Port of strykelang's \fBbuiltin_assert_contains\fR.
.TP
.B zassert_dies
\fBzassert_dies "CMD" [MSG]\fR \(em shell\-native variant of strykelang's \fBassert_dies\fR (which takes a code ref). Runs CMD in a scratch \fBShellExecutor\fR (inheriting the host's \fBzsh_compat\fR / \fBbash_compat\fR / \fBposix_mode\fR); passes iff the command exits non\-zero or errors. Use to assert that a misuse correctly fails.
.TP
.B zassert_eq
\fBzassert_eq A B [MSG]\fR \(em string equality. Like every \fBzassert_*\fR builtin, returns 0 on pass / 1 on fail and bumps the per\-block counters that \fBztest_run\fR reads.
.TP
.B zassert_err
\fBzassert_err V [MSG]\fR \(em pass when V is shell\-falsy (empty *or* literal "0"). Convenient for asserting on \fB$?\fR after a command, or on a string that should be empty.
.TP
.B zassert_false
\fBzassert_false V [MSG]\fR \(em alias of \fBzassert_err\fR.
.TP
.B zassert_ge
\fBzassert_ge A B [MSG]\fR \(em pass iff numeric A >= B. Inputs are \fBtrim().parse::<f64>()\fR'd; non\-numeric inputs default to 0.
.TP
.B zassert_gt
\fBzassert_gt A B [MSG]\fR \(em pass iff numeric A > B.
.TP
.B zassert_le
\fBzassert_le A B [MSG]\fR \(em pass iff numeric A <= B.
.TP
.B zassert_lt
\fBzassert_lt A B [MSG]\fR \(em pass iff numeric A < B.
.TP
.B zassert_match
\fBzassert_match PATTERN STRING [MSG]\fR \(em regex match. PATTERN is compiled with the \fBregex\fR crate (Rust regex syntax, not POSIX BRE/ERE \(em so \fB\ed\fR/\fB\ew\fR/\fB\es\fR work, no backreferences). Fails (rather than panics) on a bad pattern.
.TP
.B zassert_ne
\fBzassert_ne A B [MSG]\fR \(em string inequality.
.TP
.B zassert_near
\fBzassert_near A B [EPS [MSG]]\fR \(em floating\-point approximate equality: pass iff \fB|A \- B| <= EPS\fR. EPS defaults to \fB1e\-9\fR. Right for asserting on math results without burning on the last few ULPs.
.TP
.B zassert_ok
\fBzassert_ok V [MSG]\fR \(em pass when V is shell\-truthy (non\-empty *and* not literal "0"). Use \fBzassert_eq $? 0 ...\fR for command\-success assertions; reserve \fBzassert_ok\fR for assertions on populated strings or non\-zero counts.
.TP
.B zassert_true
\fBzassert_true V [MSG]\fR \(em alias of \fBzassert_ok\fR.
.TP
.B zbuild
Bake one or more shell scripts into a standalone executable that runs them in order under one shell. \fBzbuild \-\-in a.zsh b.zsh \-\-out app\fR; \fB\-\-native\fR compiles to native code with Cranelift.
.TP
.B zcache
Inspect and manage the zshrs caches: daemon shards, the catalog and the caches the shell writes itself. \fBzcache info\fR, \fBzcache clean [all|shards|shard NAME|index|shell|log|catalog|stats|zwc|zcompdump|legacy] [\-\-dry\-run]\fR, \fBzcache verify\fR, \fBzcache view TARGET\fR, \fBzcache export\fR, \fBzcache import\fR, \fBzcache daemon status|stop|restart\fR.
.TP
.B zcmd\-result
Responder side of \fBzsend \-\-wait\fR: report the exit status and output of a command a \fBcmd:execute\fR event delivered. \fBzcmd\-result \-\-id DELIVERY_ID [\-\-exit\-code N] [\-\-stdout TEXT] [\-\-stderr TEXT]\fR.
.TP
.B zcomplete
Ask the daemon's \fBcomplete\fR op for candidates (commands, handlers, history) matching a command\-line prefix and print the JSON reply. \fBzcomplete PREFIX [\-\-limit N]\fR.
.TP
.B zhistory
Read and write the daemon's cross\-shell history store (history.db). \fBzhistory append LINE [\-\-exit\-code N] [\-\-cwd DIR]\fR, \fBzhistory query [\-\-filter PAT] [\-\-mode match|fts|like|prefix|exact] [\-\-cwd DIR] [\-\-limit N]\fR, \fBzhistory count\fR.
.TP
.B zlock
Named cross\-process locks held by the daemon; a lock is released when its holder dies. \fBzlock try NAME\fR, \fBzlock acquire NAME [\-\-timeout S]\fR, \fBzlock release NAME TOKEN\fR, \fBzlock list\fR.
.TP
.B zlog
Inspect and manage the zshrs log files under ~/.zshrs. \fBzlog\fR (newest log path), \fBzlog tail [\-n N] [\-f] [shell|daemon|recorder]\fR, \fBzlog grep REGEX\fR, \fBzlog clear\fR, \fBzlog stats\fR, \fBzlog level DIRECTIVE\fR, \fBzlog rotate\fR.
.TP
.B zpublish
Publish an event on the daemon's pub/sub bus from this shell's scope. \fBzpublish TOPIC [DATA | \-\-json JSON]\fR; subscribers receive it via \fBzsubscribe\fR.
.TP
.B zsleep
Sleep for a possibly fractional number of seconds. \fBzsleep 0.25\fR; exactly one argument, no options.
.TP
.B zsource
Ask the daemon's compiled\-file registry whether a sourced file's cached body is a hit, stale or a miss. \fBzsource PATH\fR, \fBzsource \-\-stat PATH\fR.
.TP
.B zsuggest
Ask the daemon for a suggestion completing a command\-line prefix. \fBzsuggest PREFIX [\-\-cwd DIR]\fR.
.TP
.B zsync
Move canonical subsystem state (aliases, functions, options, ...) between this shell and the daemon. \fBzsync up SUBSYSTEM KEY VALUE\fR, \fBzsync up \-\-all\fR, \fBzsync pull SUBSYSTEM\fR, \fBzsync diff SUBSYSTEM \-\-overlay JSON\fR, \fBzsync watch SUBSYSTEM...\fR.
.TP
.B ztest_run
Print the per\-block test summary (green OK All N passed / red FAIL M of N failed) and roll the per\-block \fBztest_pass_count\fR / \fBztest_fail_count\fR / \fBztest_skip_count\fR into the run\-wide \fB_total\fR counters, then zero the per\-block counters so a second test block in the same file starts fresh. A sticky \fBztest_run_failed\fR flag latches when any block reports failures \(em the \fB\-\-ztest\fR worker reads it to exit non\-zero even if the script ends with \fBexit 0\fR. Alias: \fBrun_tests\fR. Returns 0 on all\-pass, 1 if any assertion failed.
.TP
.B ztest_skip
\fBztest_skip MSG\fR \(em bump the per\-block skip counter and emit a yellow SKIP marker on stderr. Typical use: gate an assertion behind a compat condition using shell short\-circuit (\fB(( cond )) || { ztest_skip "needs X"; continue; }\fR).
.TP
.B zunsubscribe
Cancel a \fBzsubscribe\fR subscription. \fBzunsubscribe SCOPE.TOPIC\fR or \fBzunsubscribe \-\-id SUBSCRIPTION_ID\fR.
.TP
.B zuntag
Remove tags from this shell's daemon session, or every tag with \fB\-\-all\fR, and print the remaining list. Inverse of \fBztag\fR.
.TP
.B zwhere
Query the daemon's definitions catalog for which shell defined an alias, function, parameter, key binding, ... and where. \fBzwhere [KIND] [NAME] [\-\-prefix P] [\-\-shell\-id N] [\-\-limit N]\fR; \fBzwhere \-\-kinds\fR lists the kinds.
